INT. CORBIN FARMHOUSE - CELLAR - NIGHT

Dirt floor. Jars of preserves. One bare bulb on a cord, swinging.

CORBIN, 50s, in a sweat-stained flannel, holds a lantern high. ALMA, 40s, his wife, grips the cellar ladder. JONAH PIKE, 20s, the hired hand, backs against a shelf. SADIE, 9, hugs a doll and stares at the far wall.

Something scratches. Behind the wall.

Corbin lowers the lantern.

CORBIN
Rats.

ALMA
Rats don't knock.

Three slow knocks. From the other side.

Jonah slides along the shelf. A jar tips. Smashes.

JONAH
(whisper)
Sir. We should go up.

CORBIN
Quiet.

Sadie walks to the wall. Presses her ear to the dirt.

ALMA
Sadie. Come here.

Sadie doesn't turn.

SADIE
It knows my name.

Alma lets go of the ladder. Corbin swings the lantern toward the sound. A thin crack runs up the plaster.

The bulb goes out.

Darkness. Breathing. Four of them.

Something wet drags along the floor.
